---
title: Braised brisket with onions
servings: 8
yield: 8 to 10 servings
tags: [jewish, beef, braise, hanukkah, passover, holiday, make-ahead]
cuisine: jewish
time: 4 hours
source: Classic Brisket with Onions by Jennifer Segal, Once Upon a Chef, https://www.onceuponachef.com/recipes/onion-braised-beef-brisket.html
---

Heat the oven to 350°F (175°C) with a rack in the middle.

Season both sides of the @brisket{5-6%lb}(first-cut or flat-cut, trimmed to a thin layer of fat) with the @kosher salt{1.5%tbsp} and @ground black pepper{1%tsp}. Dust it lightly with the @all-purpose flour{2%tbsp} and shake off the excess.

Heat the @vegetable oil{3%tbsp} over medium-high heat in a #heavy roasting pan{} or enamelled cast-iron pot that just fits the brisket and carrots. Sear the brisket ~{5-7%minutes} a side until well browned. Set it on a platter.

Add the @yellow onions{8}(medium, sliced 1/2 inch thick) to the pan and cook ~{15%minutes}, stirring and scraping up the browned bits, until soft and golden. Add a few spoons of water if the bits start to burn.

Turn off the heat. Lay the brisket, fat side up, on the onions with its juices. Spread the @tomato paste{3%tbsp} over the meat and scatter the @garlic{3%cloves}(roughly chopped) and @carrots{6}(cut in large diagonal chunks) around it.

Cover very tightly with foil or a lid and bake ~{1.5%hours}.

Lift the brisket onto a board and slice it across the grain 1/8 to 1/4 inch thick. Lay the slices back in the pan, overlapping at an angle. Cover tightly again.

Lower the oven to 325°F (165°C) and cook ~{1.75-2.5%hours} more, until fork-tender.

If the sauce looks greasy, pour it into a bowl, let the fat rise, skim it off and pour the sauce back over. Scatter with @parsley{}(chopped, optional).

> It is better made a day ahead. For Passover, use matzo cake meal in place of the flour.
